Player: This Atomon seems different from Sodium and Neon.

Player: I wonder how many electrons it has in its outermost energy level.

Player: What is the electron arrangement of Chlorine?

- 2,8,7
  Player: Correct! Chlorine has seventeen electrons arranged as 2,8,7.

- 2,8,1
  Player: Not quite. That is the electron arrangement of Sodium. Chlorine has seventeen electrons.

- 2,8,8
  Player: Not quite. That is the arrangement Chlorine can achieve after gaining one electron.

Player: So Chlorine has seven valence electrons!

Player: It needs one more electron to complete its outermost energy level.

Player: Dr. Mundo explained that atoms can gain electrons to achieve a more stable arrangement.

Player: Chlorine can gain one electron and become a negatively charged ion called Cl−.

Player: Its electron arrangement then becomes 2,8,8.

Player: Now I understand!

Player: Sodium can lose one electron, while Chlorine can gain one.

Player: Their electron arrangements can become more stable through these changes.

[signal arg="quest_notify:quest_chain_002_01:observe_chlorine"]

Player: I've finished observing the three Atomons. I should return to Dr. Mundo and share what I discovered.